    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two ornithologists of starkly different approach, Alexander Wilson and John James Audubon, dominated American ornithology in the early nineteenth century, a time when America was eager to make its name on the world stage.  Their differences underscored opposing forces in American intellectualism: Wilson was a traditional scientist focused on measurements, whereas Audubon was an iconoclastic woodsman who combined field observation with dramatic artistry. Their rivalry came to a head in 1824 when Audubon sought membership in the Academy of Natural Sciences in Philadelphia, Wilson's home and the center of American ornithology, where Audubon sought support for publishing his life’s work, an avian visual index titled The Birds of America. Audubon’s rejection in Philadelphia forced him to Britain, which welcomed this backwoods talent and published his images to acclaim. There, Audubon re-energized British bird culture and even inspired Charles Darwin’s early deliberations on evolution. This thesis illustrates the pitfalls of the early Americans’ orthodox scientific practice that ironically did not lead to the explosive Darwinian advances they so intensely desired.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
